---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import { personStructuredData } from "../lib/structured-data";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About / Robert Jackson"
  description="About Robert Jackson, rwjblue, and N1RWJ: software, open source, amateur radio, notes, projects, and contact links."
  canonicalPath="/about/"
  image="/images/pota/2026-06-04-sachuest-point-national-wildlife-refuge-pota/operator-rocky-shoreline.jpg"
  imageAlt="Robert Jackson, N1RWJ, at a rocky shoreline during a portable radio activation."
  structuredData={personStructuredData}
>
  <section class="about-hero page-intro">
    <div>
      <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
      <h1>Robert Jackson</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        I am Robert Jackson: rwjblue online, N1RWJ on the air, a software
        developer by trade, and an inveterate builder of systems for learning
        things the long way around.
      </p>
      <p>
        This site is my public notebook. It holds software and open source work,
        but lately it also holds the story of returning to amateur radio after
        nearly two decades away—and discovering that the part I once found
        hardest is now the part I enjoy most.
      </p>
    </div>
    <figure class="about-portrait">
      <img
        src="/images/pota/2026-06-04-sachuest-point-national-wildlife-refuge-pota/operator-rocky-shoreline.jpg"
        alt="Robert Jackson, N1RWJ, at a rocky shoreline during a portable radio activation."
      />
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="story-start-title">
    <div class="story-marker" aria-hidden="true">
      <span>01</span>
      <strong>1998</strong>
    </div>
    <div class="story-copy">
      <p class="section-label">The first signal</p>
      <h2 id="story-start-title">It started between the library shelves.</h2>
      <p>
        I grew up in Compton, Illinois, and worked at the local library as a
        teenager. While shelving books I found the ARRL's <cite>Now You're
        Talking!</cite>, a guide to earning an amateur radio license. Something
        about being able to build a station and talk beyond the edge of a small
        town caught hold of me.
      </p>
      <p>
        My grandfather—Papa—lived in Montgomery, near Aurora. He encouraged the
        project and drove me toward Chicago for my exam. I sailed through the
        Technician written test and failed the Morse test: I had memorized the
        order of a Gordon West practice tape instead of learning the sounds.
        After going back and learning the code for real, I earned my first
        license in 1998 as KB9SMK.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="story-ocala-title">
    <div class="story-marker" aria-hidden="true">
      <span>02</span>
      <strong>1999</strong>
    </div>
    <div class="story-copy">
      <p class="section-label">Ocala</p>
      <h2 id="story-ocala-title">A radio life, then a long quiet.</h2>
      <p>
        I moved to Ocala, Florida, in 1999 and earned my General there. A used
        Yaesu FT-100D opened up HF alongside the local repeaters. I did not yet
        have a community around the hobby, but I had a station and a great deal
        to explore.
      </p>
      <p>
        Then ordinary life got wonderfully full. I married in 2006; our first
        son arrived in January 2008 and our second followed in 2009. Work and
        family displaced radio, and I mistakenly thought an upgrade had reset
        my license term. The license expired. Later, when we moved from Ocala
        to Hope, Rhode Island, my wife packed the radio and the rest of the
        station into a Rubbermaid bin, taped it shut, and added silica gel. It
        waited in the garage for roughly twelve years.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="story-return-title">
    <div class="story-marker" aria-hidden="true">
      <span>03</span>
      <strong>2025</strong>
    </div>
    <div class="story-copy">
      <p class="section-label">The return</p>
      <h2 id="story-return-title">My son found the old station.</h2>
      <p>
        In 2025 my oldest son, then eighteen, found the bin while making room
        to work out in the garage. Opening it brought back the FT-100D, the
        antennas, and a hobby I had assumed belonged to an earlier version of
        me. I retested, became KC1YDM on August 6, and received the vanity call
        N1RWJ later that month.
      </p>
      <p>
        My brother Jon, now KC1YED, helped install an attic end-fed half-wave
        and a VHF antenna on August 16. By Christmas my wife had surprised me
        with a Yaesu FTDX10. What began as reviving an old radio quickly became
        a family-supported return to the air.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="story-section" aria-labelledby="story-now-title">
    <div class="story-marker" aria-hidden="true">
      <span>04</span>
      <strong>Now</strong>
    </div>
    <div class="story-copy">
      <p class="section-label">Outside again</p>
      <h2 id="story-now-title">Radio became a way to go places.</h2>
      <p>
        Parks on the Air gave the return a shape. It supplied a destination,
        a short exchange, and a reason to keep refining a portable station.
        I especially love hike-in activations, where every ounce and every
        forgotten adapter matters. CW made those outings quieter. I can sit
        still with a paddle while deer wander within thirty feet, connected to
        people far away without shouting into the trees.
      </p>
      <p>
        I earned Amateur Extra in March 2026 and made my first CW contact five
        weeks later. Since then, learning Morse has become less about speed and
        more about confidence, recovery, and community. The fuller version is
        in <a href="/radio/cw-journey/">my CW journey</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section-block wide" aria-labelledby="about-work-title">
    <div class="section-heading">
      <p class="section-label">The notebook</p>
      <h2 id="about-work-title">What lives here</h2>
    </div>
    <div class="split-list">
      <div>
        <h3>Software</h3>
        <p>
          Tooling, debugging notes, open source maintenance, CLI design, and
          small decisions that make systems easier to operate.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Radio</h3>
        <p>
          <a href="/radio/">Portable HF, CW, and POTA</a>, plus station design,
          antennas, logging, and field workflows from N1RWJ.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div>
        <h3>Projects</h3>
        <p>
          Active project logs, plans, experiments, and context that does not fit
          cleanly into one-off notes.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="bridge" aria-labelledby="contact-title">
    <p class="section-label">Contact</p>
    <h2 id="contact-title">Where to find me</h2>
    <p>
      The best public contact routes are the profiles below. Email is available
      through my GitHub and QRZ profiles.
    </p>
    <nav class="path-links" aria-label="Contact links">
      <a href="https://github.com/rwjblue" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
        GitHub
      </a>
      <a href="https://www.qrz.com/db/N1RWJ" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
        QRZ
      </a>
      <a href="https://bsky.app/profile/rwjblue.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">
        Bluesky
      </a>
      <a href="https://mstdn.social/@rwjblue" target="_blank" rel="me noreferrer">
        Mastodon
      </a>
      <a href="/rss.xml">RSS</a>
    </nav>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
